// The body of `/trust#windows`. Its `<h2>` and DevTodo stay in `src/pages/trust.astro`, where the
// trust review finds the anchor, and the page renders this only in `pnpm dev` or once the published
// release has a Windows download. Same truth rule as the rest of the page, for that release.
import { policyNamed, windowsPolicyKey } from '../lib/trust-mdm'

const machinePolicyKey = `HKLM\\${windowsPolicyKey}`
const userPolicyKey = `HKCU\\${windowsPolicyKey}`
// The page's one string key and one list key, checked against the definition at build time.
const versionKey = policyNamed('MaxUpdateVersion', 'string')
const hostsKey = policyNamed('AllowedCloudAIHosts', 'stringList')
---

<h3 id="windows-secrets">Passwords and API keys</h3>
<p>
    Passwords and API keys (for SMB, SFTP, WebDAV, S3, and cloud AI) and the license key are stored in Windows
    Credential Manager, never in Cmdr's files. Each one is its own generic credential, named <code>Cmdr:</code> and
    what it's for, like <code>Cmdr:license.key</code>. They're saved for this PC only, so they never roam with a domain
    profile. If Credential Manager can't be reached, Cmdr doesn't save the secret anywhere else.
</p>

<h3 id="windows-policy">Central management (Group Policy)</h3>
<p>
    IT can set the same policies as on a Mac, as registry values under <code>{machinePolicyKey}</code> for the whole
    computer or <code>{userPolicyKey}</code> for one user, with Group Policy or any tool that writes the registry. Each
    value is named after a key in the <a href="#mdm-keys">list of keys</a> and does the same thing there. When both set
    the same value, the computer's wins. Only administrators and Group Policy can write under these keys, so Cmdr treats
    every value there as managed, and Settings shows it locked.
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Values</strong>: an on/off key takes a <code>REG_DWORD</code>, where 0 is off and anything else is on.
        <code>{versionKey}</code> takes a <code>REG_SZ</code> string like <code>0.52</code>.
        <code>{hostsKey}</code> takes a <code>REG_MULTI_SZ</code>, or a subkey of string values named
        <code>1</code>, <code>2</code>, and so on. Names are compared without regard to case, as Windows does. A value
        Cmdr can't read counts as the strictest setting of that key.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Changes apply while Cmdr runs</strong>: Cmdr watches these keys, and reads them again before each send.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>To check a PC</strong>, run <code>reg query "{machinePolicyKey}"</code> and
        <code>reg query "{userPolicyKey}"</code>.
    </li>
</ul>

<h3 id="windows-updates">Installing, signing, and updates</h3>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>From the Microsoft Store</strong>: Microsoft signs the package (MSIX), so Windows runs it without a
        warning. The Store delivers updates, and Cmdr's own updater never runs in a Store install.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>From getcmdr.com</strong>: the installer isn't code-signed yet, so Windows SmartScreen stops it the
        first time. Click "More info", then "Run anyway". It installs Cmdr for the current user only, in their own
        AppData folder, with no administrator rights.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Updates to a getcmdr.com install</strong> follow the same schedule, hosts, and settings as on a Mac.
        Cmdr downloads the new installer from <code>download.getcmdr.com</code> and checks its Ed25519 (minisign)
        signature against the two public keys built into the app before it uses it. The signature also names the
        version, and Cmdr refuses an installer signed for any other version, so an older genuine installer can't pass
        as a new one, and it only installs a version newer than the one running. When an update fixes a security issue,
        the restart notice says so. On Restart, Cmdr closes, the installer runs, and Cmdr opens again.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>No second signature check yet</strong>: on a Mac, Cmdr also checks the new app's Apple Developer ID
        signature. A Windows update has no code signature to check, so its minisign signature is what makes it genuine.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Turning updates off</strong> works as on a Mac: in Settings, or for everyone with the update policies
        (<a href="#windows-policy">Group Policy</a>).
    </li>
</ul>

<h3 id="windows-ai">What Ask Cmdr can't read on Windows</h3>
<p>
    As on a Mac (<a href="#ai">AI features and your files</a>), some contents never reach the AI provider, whatever
    anyone asks. On Windows, that's:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Credentials</strong>: SSH keys, cloud and tool credentials (like AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, the GitHub
        CLI, GnuPG, Docker, and Kubernetes), the files behind Windows Credential Manager and its vaults, Windows' data
        protection keys, and Bitwarden and 1Password data.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Browsers</strong>: Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Brave, Chromium, Vivaldi, Opera, Arc, Zen, and LibreWolf.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Mail</strong>: Outlook (classic and new, plus Outlook data files in Documents), the Windows Mail app,
        and Thunderbird.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Chat apps</strong>: Teams, Slack, Discord, Signal, WhatsApp, Telegram, and Element.</li>
    <li><strong>Cmdr's own data</strong>, and any file holding a private key.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    Cmdr finds these in <code>AppData\Roaming</code> and <code>AppData\Local</code> even when IT redirects those folders
    to a file server, and in a copy of a Windows profile on another drive or a NAS. Their names can still reach the
    provider.
</p>

<h3 id="windows-storage">What is stored on the PC</h3>
<p>
    A getcmdr.com install keeps its data (settings, histories, AI chats, the log of file operations, and the security
    event log) in <code>%APPDATA%\com.veszelovszki.cmdr</code>, and its drive index, logs, and other caches in
    <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\com.veszelovszki.cmdr</code>. Both are in the user's own profile. With a roaming profile,
    Windows copies the first folder to the profile server along with the rest of <code>AppData\Roaming</code>; the
    drive index and logs stay on the PC. With BitLocker on, Windows encrypts both at rest along with the rest of the
    drive. Cmdr adds no second layer of its own.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Removing Cmdr and all its data</strong>: in Cmdr, open Settings › Updates &amp; privacy and click
    <strong>Delete all Cmdr data</strong>. It removes both folders and every <code>Cmdr:</code> credential from
    Credential Manager, then quits, and leaves your own files alone. Then uninstall Cmdr in Windows Settings › Apps.
    Already uninstalled it? Delete the two folders by hand, then open Credential Manager › Windows Credentials and
    remove the generic credentials whose names start with <code>Cmdr:</code>.
</p>

<h3 id="windows-privacy">Usage stats, crash reports, and license checks</h3>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Usage stats, error reports, and feedback</strong> work as described <a href="#network">above</a>, with
        the Windows version where a Mac sends its macOS version.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Crash reports</strong> cover a crash that Cmdr's Rust code detects as it happens (a panic). Unlike on a
        Mac, a crash in native code, inside Windows or a system library, isn't reported yet. A report never sends more
        than the list above.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>License checks</strong> send a one-way hash (SHA-256) of the <code>MachineGuid</code> that Windows Setup
        writes, where a Mac hashes its hardware UUID. Reinstalling Windows makes it a new device.
    </li>
</ul>
